#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133507 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6f6a3fd-67df-5671-aa5d-08471b2ce1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Positive Whole Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "62c086b3-028f-528b-acd5-7bdc204dcb23" :question/math-academy-id "e-12960" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $7^2$." :question/worked-solution "To work out $7^2$, we multiply $7$ by itself:\n\n$$\n7^2 = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eece577d-72b8-5a0d-89fd-1cfa63bf73fb" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "4a393145-3e63-532b-a288-775fbc7acd15" :question/math-academy-id "e-12961" :question/problem "Find the square of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "To work out\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwe multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nby itself:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^2 = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 = \\dfrac 1 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e8fb12-e59a-5aba-a9e8-2f872a89b915" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Negative Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "55e8797b-62bd-5515-81a5-e1c13249c1cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12962" :question/problem "What is the value of $(-4)^2$?" :question/worked-solution "To work out $(-4)^2$, we multiply $-4$ by itself:\n\n$$\n(-4)^2 = (-4) \\cdot (-4) = 16\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28f4ffc-5168-5e71-9d77-ae313dd462ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Square of a Decimal"}] :question/id #uuid "c07254e9-d6a6-55b1-bab7-4fe453c8cc69" :question/math-academy-id "e-12964" :question/problem "What is the square of $-2.5$?" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $(-2.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-2.5)^2 = (-2.5)\\cdot (-2.5) = (2.5)\\cdot (2.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}2[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ + & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0; 6 \\mid 2 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n25 \\times 25 = 625\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in the first factor ($2.5$) and $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in the second factor ($2.5$), so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $625$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(-2.5)^2 = 6.25\\,\n$$"}]}
